<p>Winter Storm Uri was the most damaging of these for property. Extended power and water outages across Texas meant many homes could not be kept warm, and burst pipes were widespread once temperatures rose. You can look up other events on our <a href="/storm-history/">storm history</a> page.</p>

<h2>What Freeze Damage Looks Like</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Burst supply lines</strong> in attics, exterior walls, and under sinks on outside walls.</li>
<li><strong>Water damage below the break</strong>: collapsed ceilings, wet insulation, swollen cabinets, buckled wood floors, and soaked carpet.</li>
<li><strong>Damaged fixtures and equipment</strong>: water heaters in garages or attics, outdoor spigots, irrigation backflow devices, and fire sprinkler lines in commercial buildings.</li>
<li><strong>Ice damage</strong>: limbs and trees falling on roofs, damaged gutters, and broken carports under the weight of ice.</li>
<li><strong>Hidden leaks</strong> that appear days or weeks later inside walls or under the slab.</li>
</ul>

