<h2>Policy Conditions Insurers Look At</h2>
<h3>Heat and draining conditions</h3>
<p>Many policies limit freeze coverage unless the owner used reasonable care to maintain heat in the building, or shut off the water supply and drained the system. These conditions often matter most for vacant homes, rentals between tenants, and buildings under renovation. During Uri, many owners lost power or water service through no fault of their own; how the policy wording applies to those facts can be a point of disagreement.</p>
<h3>Sudden vs. gradual</h3>
<p>Policies generally cover sudden and accidental discharge of water but exclude long-term seepage or leakage. A pipe that bursts during a freeze is usually sudden. A small crack that leaked quietly for months after the freeze may be treated as gradual. Report new leaks promptly to keep that line clear.</p>
<h3>The pipe vs. the damage</h3>
<p>Many policies pay for the water damage caused by a burst pipe and for tearing out and replacing parts of the building to reach it, but treat the pipe repair itself differently. Ask the insurer to show how it split these items.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>A Caution About Slab and Foundation Leaks</h2>
<p>Most San Antonio homes sit on slab-on-grade foundations, and the area's expansive clay soils shrink and swell with moisture. After a freeze, some owners discover a leak under the slab, along with cracks in floors or walls. Be careful with these claims. Policies commonly exclude earth movement, settling, and foundation cracking, and they may exclude the cost to repair the leaking pipe. Some policies do cover the cost to access a covered under-slab leak and the resulting water damage inside the home. Whether foundation movement was caused by the leak, by the soil, or by something else is a technical question that usually needs a licensed plumber's leak report and, often, an engineer. We will not tell you a foundation claim is covered when the policy and evidence do not support it.</p>

<h2>Documenting a Freeze Claim</h2>
<ol>
<li>Shut off the water at the main and photograph the break before repairs.</li>
<li>Keep the broken section of pipe if the plumber removes it.</li>
<li>Get a written plumber's report stating where and why the pipe failed.</li>
<li>Start drying right away and keep the mitigation company's moisture logs.</li>
<li>Write down the dates of any CPS Energy power outage or SAWS water outage and what you did to protect the home.</li>
<li>Photograph contents before discarding anything.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> has more detail. If water is the main issue, see <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Rentals, Vacant Homes, and Commercial Buildings</h2>
<p>Freeze losses at properties nobody is living in raise extra questions. Many policies reduce or exclude coverage for freezing in a vacant or unoccupied building unless heat was maintained or the water was shut off and drained, and some define &ldquo;vacant&rdquo; by a set number of days. Landlords should know how their policy treats a unit between tenants. For commercial buildings, a frozen fire sprinkler line can release a large amount of water quickly, and business income coverage may matter as much as the building repair. See <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Preparing for the Next Hard Freeze</h2>
<p>A few low-cost steps reduce the chance of a claim and help show reasonable care if one happens: know where your main shut-off valve is, insulate exposed pipes in attics and on exterior walls, cover outdoor spigots and backflow devices, open sink cabinets on outside walls, and keep a dated record of what you did. Our <a href="/weather/">San Antonio weather</a> page is a good place to watch for freeze warnings.</p>

<h2>How a Public Adjuster Can Help With Freeze Damage</h2>
<p>San Antonio Public Adjuster represents policyholders on freeze and burst pipe claims. That may include reviewing the freezing and water damage provisions in your policy, documenting damage room by room, preparing a line-item estimate that includes access, tear-out, and drying, and responding to insurer arguments about heat, timing, or gradual leakage. If the claim was paid but left items out, an <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplement</a> may be appropriate; if it was denied, see <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a>. We cannot guarantee a result. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
